# Per-step fetch study: unsloth/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, base int1, prefill fetch k=128, self-written targets (T=0)

31 pauses, 43.5 teacher-forced steps per pause on average (cap 64). Targets: the bf16 model's own next message (greedy, seed 0 + per-pause offset; bf16 prefill of history and tool output; 20-64 tokens, mean 43.5), saved in self_targets.jsonl; every row is teacher-forced on them. dNLL in nats per target token against the per-step bf16 row. Base: int1 history, block 16 (SpeCache's modified 1-bit quantizer); block 0, the recent 64 tokens and the trailing partial block are bf16. Tool output prefilled over the int1 history with a per-chunk top-128 fetch (fetch rows; the uniform int1 row and fetch_all_noprefill without); uniform rows over their own precision, the bf16 row over bf16.

## Checks

- bf16 row vs itself: max |dNLL| 0.0e+00
- per-step vs sdpa teacher forcing, NLL difference per pause (the stop criterion): bf16 row max 0.0060 (mean 0.0017), int1 row max 0.0184 (mean 0.0046)
- uniform int1 dNLL, sdpa batched +0.4947 vs per-step +0.4952 (max per-pause |difference| 0.0170)
- largest single-token log-prob difference (reported only): bf16 0.110, int1 0.373
- 85.6 s per pause on average, 44.2 min total
- speculative token (spec rows, guess of each step's input from the real previous token): correct on 93.4% (spec@16), 94.5% (spec@32), 95.5% (spec@64), 96.8% (spec@128), 98.0% (spec@match) of steps
- speculative token (spec_chain rows, SpeCache's chained guess of the next input): correct on 30.8% (spec_chain@16), 29.6% (spec_chain@32), 35.5% (spec_chain@64), 42.5% (spec_chain@128), 55.7% (spec_chain@match) of steps
- k = match: 1,116 tokens per KV head and layer on average (min 495, max 2,110), i.e. int1 + fetched set = int2's memory
- GPU memory: measured peak 38.7 GB (allocated by PyTorch), estimated 37.1 GB

## Quality

Fetched = (layer, KV head, token) pairs fetched per step; distinct = distinct token positions per layer per step. Recall = share of each fetched set that is also in the oracle set of the same size, computed with the row's own current query. The last column compares each fetch scheme with s2 (current-step attention over base keys) at the same k. KL = KL(bf16 row || row) of the next-token distribution, nats, averaged over steps and pauses.

Noise floor (noise_ctrl: bf16 tool-output prefill + fetch-all, exactly the bf16 row in exact arithmetic, so any dNLL is bf16 rounding in the per-step path): mean +0.0045 [95% CI +0.0010, +0.0093], s.d. over pauses 0.0124, mean |dNLL| 0.0064, max |dNLL| 0.0614; top-1 0.990 (min 0.939); KL vs bf16 mean 0.0025 (max 0.0220). Per-pause scheme differences smaller than about 0.0248 (2 s.d.) are within rounding noise.

